        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
A number of waste management technologies are being developed that stabilize waste or recover resources for long-duration manned missions. The success of many waste processing resource recovery technologies thus depends on the ability of gas clean-up systems to efficiently remove contaminants produced by the waste management systems. There is reason to believe that a new approach to gas clean-up using carbon nanotubes may have significant advantages in mass, power, and volume over traditional approaches. 
<p>
Carbon nanotubes possess selective adsorptive and catalytic properties as well as high surface areas that suggest that significant improvements can be made over currently available methods for gaseous contaminant cleanup. Single walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs) are currently being studied by a team at NASA Ames Research Center for their effectiveness as catalysts and catalyst support materials for trace contaminant control. 
<p>
The research on this project at NASA Ames focuses on: purification of single walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNT); catalyst impregnation of SWCNTs; characterization and application of SWCNTs as catalysts and/or catalyst supports for toxic gas conversion; and development of a prototype reactor. The single walled carbon nanotubes synthesized by catalytic methods contain metallic impurities such as Fe, Ni, and Co in addition to various forms of carbon such as graphite, amorphous carbon, and nanoparticles. Purification involves removal of these impurities, which results in a high surface area, molecularly well-defined SWCNT. Several different methods for purification to about 0.5 percent residual metal are underway. For catalyst applications, impregnation of the SWCNTs with metals such as Rh, Pd, Pt, Ag, Ru, Nb is being conducted. Analytical information on nanostructure and composition is being accumulated for correlation with results from adsorption and conversion efficiency experiments. 
<p>
A NASA Ames research team with combined expertise in nanotechnology research and development of advanced life support systems is investigating the potential applications of single walled carbon nanotubes for trace contamination control for future manned space missions. 
<p>
